  "secondary_categories": [],
  "slug": "active-levy-differential-dynamic-7827",
  "summary": "Recent MD and polymer studies show membrane lipids, bound peptides and domain dimerization shifting conformational and assembly kinetics.",
  "synthesis": "Across these studies, assembly and conformational state are controlled by local binding partners rather than intrinsic sequence alone. Membrane lipids (PC/PI) orient HCV p7 dimers and promote early oligomerization through the first helix, while TREM2 transmembrane mutations perturb TREM2-DAP12 complex formation in multiscale simulations. An ApoC-II peptide bridges the NTD and CTD of lipoprotein lipase, coupling lid open/closed dynamics to CTD flipping. In S100B, the K55A mutation and TRTK ligand alter allosteric network behavior and Ca2+-binding affinity. For formin FH1 domains, N-terminal dimerization changes polymer dynamics in a coarse-grained model that extends kinetic actin assembly. The membrane-associated p7 and TREM2 studies address assembly most directly, while the remaining items focus on domain and allosteric dynamics.",
